WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:07.510
- Greetings everyone.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:09.150
I'm really excited to welcome

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:12.890
professor Kathleen Cruz
Gutierrez here today,

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:15.660
especially because her
work resonates with so many

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.030
of the exciting conversations
that are going on at Yale

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:22.352
and elsewhere at the intersection of

00:00:22.352 --> 00:00:23.300
environmental humanities,

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.040
Southeast Asian studies, history.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.120
And of course in my own
field of anthropology

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.670
where everyone is talking
about the entanglements

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.400
of plants and humans

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.870
as well as science and technology studies,

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.803
which Dr. Gutierrez is deeply involved in.

00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:41.170
She's working at the forefront
of all of these fields

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:43.610
and using the critical
study of the history

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.510
of botanical science to
rethink a range of assumptions

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.500
about Philippines history, ideas of region

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.060
in Southeast Asia, not
to mention just region

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.380
but how Southeast Asian has
themselves imagine the idea

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.780
of Southeast Asia itself
and the role of science

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.060
in imperial and nationalist formations.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.840
I think there's a lot
of conversations to have

00:01:07.928 --> 00:01:09.300
between faculty at UC Santa Cruz

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.690
where she has recently taken up a position

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.310
as assistant professor and
all of us here at Yale.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.980
And I hope this conversation today

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.603
will be just the first of many to come.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.430
I wanna say a little bit
about her recent dissertation

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:28.740
in my course, on the politics
of culture in Southeast Asia

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.290
we read and discussed this
brilliant piece of work

00:01:32.337 --> 00:01:34.040
The Region of Imperial Strategy.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.810
And we had a vibrant discussion
and all of us in the class

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.524
found it to be extremely multilayered.

00:01:39.524 --> 00:01:43.180
And in the course I asked
students to write book reviews

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.750
of the works we read

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.000
and I thought it would
be appropriate to quote

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
from one such review written
by Orven Mallari, who is here.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:53.860
You could see Orven there

00:01:54.778 --> 00:01:55.611
I didn't know Orven would be here

00:01:55.611 --> 00:01:58.350
but a brilliant undergraduate
student who actually

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.250
is majoring in a STEM field

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.220
but interested in the Philippines

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.320
and Southeast Asian studies.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.770
So I'm just gonna read one paragraph

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.370
I hope you don't mind Orven

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:12.240
I've just snatched a paragraph
from your brilliant review.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.510
Orven writes, "In the
region of Imperial strategy

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:20.850
Kathleen Gutierrez asks us
to move the kaleidoscope

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.572
through which we have
been viewing the history

00:02:23.572 --> 00:02:25.643
of the Philippines if only slightly

00:02:25.643 --> 00:02:30.200
in a way that brings the
unexpectedly rich story of botany

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.053
into the political history of the nation.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:38.790
In doing so we pry open an
unseen network of individuals

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:43.380
and institutions ripe
with new stories to tell.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:48.090
Seeing Filipino history bound
through Prescott's paradigm

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.820
the seemingly unconscious
division of a pre 1898

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.430
and post 1898 Philippines,
Gutierrez seeks to uproot

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.480
this tyranny of historiography
to portray the role

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.884
of scientific collaboration
and fostering the makings

00:03:03.884 --> 00:03:07.410
of an inter Imperial hegemony of thought.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.660
For Gutierrez the Philippines

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.990
is not merely a peripheral
figure in the arena

00:03:14.078 --> 00:03:15.680
of European imperialism's
decline at the start

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.990
of the 20th century,
but a central hub of it.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:24.130
The seeds of post-imperial
and post-colonial logics

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.890
were not planted in
the halls of their site

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.740
or the international botanical Congress

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.547
but within the letters sent
from the gardens of Manila."

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.900
I think it's such a beautiful evocation

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.900
of this rich tapestry of
science and technology studies,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.350
Southeast Asian studies that we encounter

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.270
in professor Gutierrez's dissertation.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.230
And so I just wanna use that as a window

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.425
to open us into today's conversation.

00:03:54.425 --> 00:03:57.687
Today's discussion will be
sort of a talk show format

00:03:57.687 --> 00:04:01.790
and so I wanna build
from Orven's introduction

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:06.790
to the dissertation there
and ask professor Gutierrez

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.900
to tell us a little bit more
about this dissertation,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.113
The Region of Imperial Strategy.

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:18.700
You describe a series of
Spanish era and an American era

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.260
botanical texts and then you
use these to make an argument

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:25.532
about the emergence of a
kind of botanical science

00:04:25.532 --> 00:04:28.810
that transforms Philippine history.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.410
So I wondered if you could
just tell us a little bit

00:04:32.402 --> 00:04:33.846
about some of these texts

00:04:33.846 --> 00:04:37.954
and some of the actors that
you so brilliantly discuss

00:04:37.954 --> 00:04:40.340
in the dissertation and
how you mobilize those

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.790
to get us to think of new ways

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.860
about the Philippines, Southeast
Asia, and other things.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.220
- Sure, thank you so much.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:49.614
And thanks for the introduction
Orven that was amazing

00:04:49.614 --> 00:04:53.300
(laughs) very generous of you
and your description of it

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.240
and I think you really
hit the nail on the head.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.700
I mean, so much of the
Genesis of the dissertation

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.570
came from this problem
what's happening around 1898?

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:06.120
Why is science especially
projected in this way

00:05:07.488 --> 00:05:10.460
in which we have sort of the
Spanish Imperial backwater

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:13.260
and then we have the us progressive period

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.443
in Philippine history.

00:05:15.443 --> 00:05:16.980
And I wanted to just investigate
it a little bit more,

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:20.230
see what was going on, see
what was actually salient

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.940
and then find where there
was something divergent

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.120
and it came down to botany.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.750
And so if you sort of muddy 1898

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.350
as the strict division and
Philippine colonial history

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:32.700
you actually see quite a bit of similarity

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.510
across the two botany projects
under the Spanish and the US.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:39.190
And so by looking at certain texts

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:41.510
I was able to find that
one of those things

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:44.040
that muddies 1898 is this preoccupation

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:47.290
with the region, this
idea of the Philippines

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.286
being a part of a floristic region.

00:05:50.286 --> 00:05:51.870
And so there were Spanish botanists

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.332
who were invested in that,
and there were US botanists

00:05:54.332 --> 00:05:55.165
invested in this as well.

00:05:55.165 --> 00:05:58.270
And so by way of texts,
one the texts I look at

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.261
during the Spanish era
is this 1883 publication

00:06:01.261 --> 00:06:03.772
by Sebastian Beadle.

00:06:03.772 --> 00:06:05.773
Who's this Catalan botanist

00:06:05.773 --> 00:06:08.920
perhaps the most famous
secular Spanish botanist

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.380
in the late 19th century
in the Philippians.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.380
It's illustrated with Regina Garcia

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.210
who's a local illustrator, a
Filipino local illustrator.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:18.790
And they're really interested

00:06:20.262 --> 00:06:21.340
in putting the Philippines flora

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:26.340
in conversation with other
flora from Island Southeast Asia

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:29.180
would become Island Southeast Asia.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.220
The same is happening in the US period

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.580
particularly with the
work of Elmer di Merrill

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:36.860
who's perhaps the most
well-known US colonial botanist

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.420
that we have in Philippine history.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:40.963
And he's working on regional flora slowly

00:06:40.963 --> 00:06:42.530
while he's in the Philippines.

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:44.710
He's looking at Indochina, he's looking at

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:47.549
the Indian sub-continent,
he's looking at Ambun

00:06:47.549 --> 00:06:49.820
in the Dutchy Cindys, and
he's putting the Philippines

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.263
in conversation with
regional flora as well.

00:06:53.249 --> 00:06:56.741
And so for me, I'm really
curious in the dissertation about

00:06:56.741 --> 00:07:00.420
what exactly is connecting
this intellectual trend.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.390
And I argue that in part
it's happening as a result of

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.636
kind of how botany is internationalizing

00:07:05.636 --> 00:07:09.600
and becoming sort of this
Neo-Imperial formation

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.150
of international collaboration.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:14.250
And one of those strategies
is through region building

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.010
and flora becomes a really
great determining platform

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:18.080
for this.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.953
In Ireland, Southeast Asia but
what we would really again.

00:07:22.255 --> 00:07:23.088
consider the broader region.

00:07:23.088 --> 00:07:25.139
You asked about people, Erik

00:07:25.139 --> 00:07:26.640
and so I divide the dissertation

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.753
through these stories
of three individuals.

00:07:28.753 --> 00:07:31.560
Regina Garcia who I
mentioned is this illustrator

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:34.060
who also is kind of known as a botanist

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:35.560
who learns it on the job

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.222
even though he doesn't
have formal training.

00:07:38.222 --> 00:07:41.040
Sebastian Vidal, the
secular Spanish botanist,

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.282
who even though his is really
famous in the Philippines

00:07:44.282 --> 00:07:47.120
at the end of the 19th century,
his career gets eclipsed

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.930
in the historiography in many ways

00:07:49.805 --> 00:07:52.960
I think because there's this
focus on US Imperial science

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.210
that clouds what's happening
during Spanish period.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.040
And then my third main
actor is Mary Strong Clemons

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:01.902
who is a US plant collector.

00:08:01.902 --> 00:08:03.720
She collects all throughout

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.330
what would become Southeast Asia.

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:07.210
And so I follow her career a little bit

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:08.420
through the Philippians.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.660
And by looking at her
career I'm able to open up

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:13.700
conversations about local
collectors in the Philippines

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.046
who were also contributing
to botany at the time.

00:08:16.046 --> 00:08:18.790
And what I love about these people is that

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.340
their careers overlap.

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:22.010
Some of them collaborate with one another,

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:24.950
some of them mention each
other in their correspondence,

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:27.695
and they really help
again, sort of muddy 1898.

00:08:27.695 --> 00:08:31.690
I think when you follow
actors who are just kind of

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:36.670
pushing aside periodization
for a moment in our narratives

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:39.800
and allowing us to see what
other dynamic configurations

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:42.053
are happening intellectually at the time.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.790
- [Erik] Thank you, Kathleen.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:50.610
I have this book actually that

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:52.320
because when I read your dissertation,

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.160
I looked in the Yale library.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.860
This is the one by Vidal
(speaks in foreign language).

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:03.562
And it's the one and I'm
also sharing with the group

00:09:03.562 --> 00:09:05.898
the names of those people,
because I have it in my notes

00:09:05.898 --> 00:09:07.278
from reading your dissertation

00:09:07.278 --> 00:09:08.830
so people can have those names.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:10.550
But if I open this, I'm just curious

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.113
if I open this and show it to the group.

00:09:13.958 --> 00:09:15.720
I don't know if people can see

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.623
can people see this when I share this?

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:24.110
Can you just say what it's
like to encounter this

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:27.635
in the archive and how
opening a book like this

00:09:27.635 --> 00:09:31.798
reveals something about
Philippine history.

00:09:31.798 --> 00:09:35.130
I was trying to communicate this

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:37.497
with the students in my class

00:09:37.497 --> 00:09:39.556
but I hadn't yet got the
book from the library

00:09:39.556 --> 00:09:40.690
it took a couple of days to get it.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:42.030
And I'm just curious like

00:09:42.925 --> 00:09:45.730
there's these archival materials, right?

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:48.297
That are in all these
libraries around the world

00:09:48.297 --> 00:09:51.280
and hidden in them as these
kind of hidden stories.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.051
I wonder if you could
unpack that a little bit-

00:09:54.051 --> 00:09:54.884
- Sure.

00:09:54.884 --> 00:09:57.284
- [Erik] In the encounter
with one of the plates

00:09:57.284 --> 00:09:58.720
because there's something
in your dissertation as well

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.580
the way in which you
attend to the drawings.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.070
And there's like a kind
of almost an archive

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:08.440
inscribed into the
drawings themselves, right?

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:09.450
- Yeah.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.440
- [Erik] I wonder if you could
expound on that a little bit.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:11.300
- Definitely, yeah.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:13.190
Thank you so much for
bringing the text out.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:16.450
And you know, I have to admit
that part of my investigation

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:20.430
began with Vidal, he sort of
left behind the best archive

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.340
from this period in Philippine history

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:24.970
and it's all in Madrid.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.933
But what I was starting to
discover as I was looking through

00:10:27.933 --> 00:10:30.340
Vidal's archive was this
sort of peripheral character

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:32.913
who would just emerge this Riheno Garcia,

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.980
who he collected with, who took
him around the Philippines,

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:40.377
somehow his resume, right?

00:10:40.377 --> 00:10:41.630
Well, we would have the
resume in Spanish service

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:43.980
appears in Madrid which is really unlikely

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:46.293
for a Philippine born in Filipino.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.040
And so I kind of started
following the breadcrumbs

00:10:50.992 --> 00:10:53.193
through that and Garcia's
name appears, right?

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:56.080
In this particular text
as the lead illustrator.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.520
And it was only through
following Garcia that I realized

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:01.460
that there was this
larger story to be told

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:05.310
about this one locally born
individual who starts off

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.200
as an artist who gets hired
at the botanical garden

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.210
of Manila in part because
he needs to help raise

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:13.430
his younger siblings.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:15.380
Put's society's fine arts degree

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.637
and kind of goes in
and works in the garden

00:11:17.637 --> 00:11:20.440
and becomes really the lead illustrator

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.270
for the Spanish colonial project

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.130
I mean the last decades
of the 19th century.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:28.110
And so process wise, you
know, I'm looking at this text

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:29.930
for much more significance

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:32.010
than I think that I
had originally gone in.

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:34.820
And I was telling Erik
this, that I would spend

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.270
maybe like 30 minutes to an hour

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:39.290
just looking at a plate,
figuring what was so different

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.023
about it and unique at the time.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:45.130
And only after maybe consulting
with another art historian

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:46.700
was I realizing that Garcia was doing

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:49.200
something really artistically different

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.770
with botanical plates
compared to his contemporaries

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:56.480
who were synopsis, the synopsis genre

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.500
in other locations, other countries

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:00.280
and certainly other colonial terrain.

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:03.610
He's doing things that's
allowing him to deploy

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:05.310
his sort of fine arts acumen.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:08.950
So if you look at the
plate that Erik showed,

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:11.596
plants are overlapping, they're kind of

00:12:11.596 --> 00:12:14.277
there's a 3D element to it and vibe

00:12:14.277 --> 00:12:17.170
a kind of a lushness with
the page that's coming out.

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:21.970
So very different from a linear
organization of plant life

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:23.780
or even insect life that you're seeing

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:25.900
in contemporary publications.

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:28.670
And this kind of lack of linearity,

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:32.380
this ability to show plant life
almost as a kind of lushness

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:33.720
of the archipelago.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.780
I think it's speaking to a
moment again where these texts

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.390
or these images are communicating

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:42.397
a different kind of Philippine
flora that's more inviting.

00:12:42.397 --> 00:12:46.670
And I think it's also reflective
of Garcia's own knowledge

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:48.110
of what's happening in the environment.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:50.530
Yeah and thank you so much Orven

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:53.997
for showing that image as well
thankfully in the archive,

00:12:53.997 --> 00:12:58.450
I was able to find Garcia's process picks

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:03.430
from sketch to the rendered
painting of a similar plant

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:06.770
to the actual image that
shows up in the synopsis.

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:10.826
And you can just see how
complex these images are

00:13:10.826 --> 00:13:14.240
Russell Monarrez, was a
really well-known historian

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:17.410
of the Philippines says that
the 1880s is known as this time

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:18.270
in Philippine history

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.800
when we see the most
important publications

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.210
to Philippine nationalism come out

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:24.993
including the Noleen by Jose Resal.

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:29.030
I actually think the synopsis
is one of those texts

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:31.950
and it's doing this in really
incredible illustrated ways

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:34.350
that we can continue to
read as its own archive.

00:13:37.090 --> 00:13:39.035
- [Erik] Thanks for that explanation.

00:13:39.035 --> 00:13:44.035
There's two discussions
in your dissertation

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.730
