00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:18.470
You have a chapter in your dissertation

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:21.400
where you're talking about
the sampaguita flower

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:26.220
and I think it would be useful
maybe to tell us a little bit

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:28.090
about what you're doing in that chapter.

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:30.380
And remember, most people
haven't read the dissertation

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:32.910
so if you could describe
what you're doing there

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:35.265
and what is the sampaguita flower

00:19:35.265 --> 00:19:39.560
and how does this offer a kind
of window into the confluence

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:42.967
of botany, nationalism, floristic regions,

00:19:42.967 --> 00:19:46.452
all of these kinds of things
that you're dealing with?

00:19:46.452 --> 00:19:47.285
- Sure, sure.

00:19:47.285 --> 00:19:48.950
Yeah and then for those
of you who have read

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:51.920
the dissertation overhauling
the chapter presently (laughs)

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:53.905
so it's changing a little bit

00:19:53.905 --> 00:19:56.300
but thanks for bringing that up, Erik.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:58.490
So the sampaguita, for those
of you who might not know

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:00.410
it's the national flower
of the Philippines

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:02.640
it's kind of the darling flower.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.220
It has a lot of popular significance

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:07.907
in the Philippines and you sort of see it

00:20:07.907 --> 00:20:10.590
pedaled on the streets of Manila.

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.443
It comes up in a lot of
cultural production presently

00:20:13.443 --> 00:20:16.595
both in diaspora and in
the Philippines itself.

00:20:16.595 --> 00:20:19.210
So I'm really curious in that chapter

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:21.930
about really kind of taking
that sampaguita to task

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:24.360
and just questioning for a moment

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:27.340
that it's always been in
the Philippine imaginary

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:29.970
really trying to locate when it emerges

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:34.106
and gains kind of that power,
that political valence.

00:20:34.106 --> 00:20:36.960
And I locate that in the late 19th century

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:40.320
where I start to see I'm able to identify

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:45.060
at the intersection of
intellectual thought in Manila,

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:49.039
scientific production and
botany and cultural production,

00:20:49.039 --> 00:20:54.039
how the sampaguita becomes this
emblem for the protonation.

00:20:54.333 --> 00:20:58.320
And so I argue kind of in
the revision of the chapter

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:00.930
that it does so because of it's gendered

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:04.140
and gender inequalities that get mapped on

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:07.220
to the nation itself or the nation form.

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:10.720
The sampaguita at the time
really becomes the stand-in

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:12.333
for ideal femininity.

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:16.710
She is light-skinned, she's
fragrant, she's in bloom,

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:19.163
she's in her prime, she's reproductive.

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:23.100
Historian. Raquel Rayez
has looked at sort of this

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:25.790
before and how gender plays
a really important role

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:29.090
in how the mother country of
the Philippians gets developed

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:30.800
specifically in illustrado

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:32.877
or enlightened intellectual thinking

00:21:32.877 --> 00:21:35.670
at the end of the 19th century.

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:38.940
What I think you can add
to that though is botany

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:40.760
and this flower and I actually would argue

00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:43.280
that ideal femininity comes by way

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:45.935
of like looking at a flower as well.

00:21:45.935 --> 00:21:47.910
And so there's sort of
this co-constitution

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:50.550
of ideal femininity and
the idea of the flower

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:54.040
and how this gets repurposed
for creating the archipelago

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:55.870
or archipelagic space.

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:58.340
Manila intellectuals at the
time are starting to suggest

00:21:58.340 --> 00:22:00.310
that this flower is ubiquitous,

00:22:00.310 --> 00:22:02.120
it's everywhere in the archipelago.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:03.300
You don't even have to describe it

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:04.640
because you just have
to show it to someone

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:05.897
and they'll be able to name it

00:22:05.897 --> 00:22:07.770
and they write on it like this

00:22:07.770 --> 00:22:09.140
and they're distinguishing it from like

00:22:09.140 --> 00:22:10.707
the Peninsular Jasmine they're saying that

00:22:10.707 --> 00:22:12.422
it's something very specific

00:22:12.422 --> 00:22:14.511
it only grows in the Philippines.

00:22:14.511 --> 00:22:18.960
Botany publications are you
know, producing information

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:19.793
on the plant.

00:22:19.793 --> 00:22:21.520
But if you actually look at the literature

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:23.690
many of the observations
around the sampaguita

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:24.990
are quite localized.

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:27.360
They're in Manila, they're
in parts of the Visayas

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.139
in the central region.

00:22:29.139 --> 00:22:31.780
And yet it's starting
to come into dominance

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:33.380
as sort of this popular emblem.

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:35.540
And so I think in the late 19th century

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:37.800
that's when it's really
starting to take hold

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:39.870
when nationalists are seeing this

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:44.270
as a unique subject or object
to start mapping, right?

00:22:44.270 --> 00:22:46.711
And then eventually, the
sampaguita gets declared

00:22:46.711 --> 00:22:50.770
the national flower in 1934
and we've had it since.

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:53.030
And so I think having
this history is important

00:22:53.030 --> 00:22:55.380
especially when we
invest, I think in sort of

00:22:55.380 --> 00:23:00.320
nationalist projects that take
on plant life as emblematic

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:01.153
of the nation.

00:23:01.153 --> 00:23:03.120
And I think there's
always a history to this

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:06.830
and never really just an
acceptance of it always been,

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:07.723
you know, for me.

00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:12.017
- [Erik] Thank you.

00:23:12.017 --> 00:23:13.500
This is so fascinating this discussion

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:17.820
and going through the way
in which even a plant itself

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:19.400
can become a new archive

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:22.805
into our understandings
of nationalism and region.

