00:26:03.650 --> 00:26:06.510
You know, I think these are
great very Philippine history,

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:08.540
specific questions so
I'm happy to answer them

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:11.860
and I'll try to be as general as possible

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:13.503
to make them pretty accessible.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:16.500
On the question on the
Vidal and his difference

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:17.927
with Quelear or Quoyar.

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:21.120
So Quoyar is an interesting figure

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:23.610
and he sort of the
naturalists par excellence

00:26:23.610 --> 00:26:27.240
and long history of
botany in the Philippians.

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:30.062
I think Vidal is a bit different
from him in the sense that

00:26:30.062 --> 00:26:31.440
Vidal is really deployed
to the Philippines

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:34.850
towards a lot more institutional building.

00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:38.320
Queyar's visit to the
Philippines, his study of flora

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:41.640
in many ways is also kind
of a commercial expedition,

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:43.160
he doesn't quite get to the point

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:44.730
of institution building, right?

00:26:44.730 --> 00:26:47.377
As Vidal does for various circumstances

00:26:47.377 --> 00:26:50.350
and Vidal is courted by
the Spanish statement

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:53.110
I think a different way
than Queyar is, right?

00:26:53.110 --> 00:26:55.177
If you especially think
of the late 18th century

00:26:55.177 --> 00:26:58.240
where the Spanish state was in terms of

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:01.090
developing scientific institutions

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:02.580
this was really kind of the realm

00:27:02.580 --> 00:27:05.930
of the Royal society still,
but by the late 19th century

00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:09.140
you see Vidal not only deployed
to the archipelago himself

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:11.810
he's coming with a team of foresters

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:14.650
of botany trained men from the peninsula.

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:16.270
His brother is in the Philippines too,

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:17.817
his uncle is in the Philippines

00:27:17.817 --> 00:27:20.410
and they're all really taking up botany

00:27:20.410 --> 00:27:21.963
in a different way I think.

00:27:23.091 --> 00:27:25.630
That light leads into your
next question about training

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:28.287
I wouldn't call it
apprenticeship so much, actually.

00:27:28.287 --> 00:27:31.460
And I can tell you why,
in the case of Garcia

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:33.440
but training is happening specifically

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:36.870
at the botanical garden of
Manila, school of agriculture.

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:39.960
They actually instituted
this pension out a program

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:42.460
to bring in students from
across the archipelago,

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:44.703
young men to study in botany and forestry

00:27:44.703 --> 00:27:47.550
to then be deployed back
across the archipelago.

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:50.630
What is unique about that is
we actually credit the pension

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:53.380
out a program to something
of the United States, right?

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:56.550
The bringing of young
people from the Philippines

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:58.778
to the United States to train,

00:27:58.778 --> 00:28:00.376
to send them back to the Philippines

00:28:00.376 --> 00:28:02.087
to take up government
posts or teaching posts.

00:28:02.087 --> 00:28:05.070
This is happening in the
Philippines under a span

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:07.800
it's just happening in a
sort of within the colony.

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:09.410
And so that training is happening,

00:28:09.410 --> 00:28:12.037
more men from the peninsula
are also coming in

00:28:12.037 --> 00:28:15.800
and they're leaving to
different parts of the islands.

00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:18.710
The reason why I wouldn't use
apprenticeship so much though

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:20.810
is because in the case of Garcia.

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:22.980
Oh gosh I actually don't
even think of him as so much

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:25.330
as an apprentice, as I
think of him in many ways

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:28.510
as someone who is able to
stay institutionally in place

00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:32.280
as a person who was local,
who had elite standing

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:35.380
but also had enough
connections to kind of stay

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:37.030
within the botany institutions

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:40.470
and outlast his Spanish contemporaries.

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:42.740
Garcia is an interesting
character because he's really

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:45.130
the longest standing person working

00:28:45.130 --> 00:28:47.390
for Spanish secular
botany me through the end

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:49.040
of the 19th century.

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:51.290
I don't really think of
that as apprenticeship

00:28:51.290 --> 00:28:53.620
and when you see him come
in to the botanical garden

00:28:53.620 --> 00:28:55.430
of Manila, he's not trained.

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:58.350
And actually Spanish
records make many critiques

00:28:58.350 --> 00:28:59.860
of him or his lack of training.

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:01.980
And there's not a lot of effort,

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:04.390
I think to necessarily put him in training

00:29:04.390 --> 00:29:06.430
or even send him back to the peninsula

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:08.593
this is something he's
learning on the job.

00:29:08.593 --> 00:29:11.740
And then I would say in
his relationship with Vidal

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:13.960
when Vidal finally arrives
in the archipelago,

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:16.820
I consider them equals
actually I consider that

00:29:16.820 --> 00:29:19.960
Vidal's Corpus really only takes shape,

00:29:19.960 --> 00:29:21.580
really only gets off the ground

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:23.400
because of what Garcia is doing.

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:26.900
Both illustrating his
texts, but also helping him

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:29.570
go around the archipelago
and collecting with him

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:31.110
along the way.

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:33.860
Vidal dies an untimely
death in the 19th century

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:37.280
and it's actually Garcia who
is responsible for collecting

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:40.771
and organizing his plant
life that's in the barium.

00:29:40.771 --> 00:29:44.290
Because he was the most familiar
with what those collecting

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:45.820
expeditions have been.

00:29:45.820 --> 00:29:48.030
And so I'd love to talk to
you a little bit more about,

00:29:48.030 --> 00:29:49.940
what's happening at the Jesuit observatory

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:52.960
and even sort of the use
of the term apprenticeship

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:55.540
'cause I think there's
something maybe different

00:29:55.540 --> 00:29:58.080
and maybe similar happening
in botany, but yeah

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:00.570
there may be something a
little bit more divergent.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:01.553
Thanks Kirby.

00:30:02.514 --> 00:30:06.340
- [Erik] Thanks Kirby for
such a provocative question

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:08.675
and Kat for such a wonderful answer.

00:30:08.675 --> 00:30:11.480
Next, we have a question from Cresa Pugh

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:13.880
and Cresa please if I've
mispronounced your name,

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:15.080
feel free to correct me.

00:30:16.355 --> 00:30:17.377
- Yeah, it's Cresa

00:30:17.377 --> 00:30:18.407
thank you so much.

00:30:18.407 --> 00:30:19.240
And thank you, Kathleen

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:21.465
for this really fascinating presentation.

00:30:21.465 --> 00:30:23.190
I have a question which is maybe

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:24.850
beyond the scope of your dissertation.

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:28.380
So I apologize in advance
of it is, but I'm wondering

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:33.110
about the role of kind
of how decoloniality

00:30:33.110 --> 00:30:37.606
or specifically anti-coloniality
appear in your work.

00:30:37.606 --> 00:30:39.633
I'm particularly curious about,

00:30:39.633 --> 00:30:44.480
to what extent have there
been attempts to kind of

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:48.323
unravel these Imperial
institutions and that you speak on?

00:30:49.300 --> 00:30:51.960
Have there been attempts
to make indigenous claims

00:30:51.960 --> 00:30:55.903
to the botanical specimens
that you referenced?

00:30:56.801 --> 00:30:58.323
I remember a couple of years ago

00:30:58.323 --> 00:31:00.560
when I was in the
Singapore botanical gardens

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:04.100
and there were these
pretty significant efforts

00:31:04.100 --> 00:31:06.810
to rename a lot of the specimens

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:10.660
from their botanical Latin
names to their indigenous names

00:31:10.660 --> 00:31:15.660
and even replanting native
trees to replace plants

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:18.450
that had been imported from the colonies.

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:21.950
And I really saw that as
a kind of decolonial move

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:24.840
and so I'm wondering how,
you know, decoloniality

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:28.050
and anti-coloniality even
show up on your work.

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:30.637
- Yeah, thanks so much
Cresa for that question.

00:31:30.637 --> 00:31:32.450
And it's really coming up much more

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:34.910
in how I'm revising
towards the manuscript.

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:36.670
I think these questions are important

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:38.800
because as compelling as sort of this

00:31:38.800 --> 00:31:41.320
Imperial internationalist narrative is

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:44.610
I'm really curious about
what's still happening locally?

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:47.160
What's being obscured
perhaps by the name botany

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:49.680
but is never actually destroyed by it.

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:51.520
And so my focus on the dissertation

00:31:51.520 --> 00:31:54.380
is really looking at that
vibrancy of botanical vernaculars

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:57.700
and by vernaculars I
don't only mean sort of

00:31:57.700 --> 00:31:59.900
linguistically how plants were called

00:31:59.900 --> 00:32:02.420
outside of Latin binomial nomenclature

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:04.123
but vernacular knowledge of plants.

00:32:04.123 --> 00:32:06.890
That even historians perhaps
aren't really looking at

00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:10.130
or haven't looked at yet completely.

00:32:10.130 --> 00:32:12.660
And so in a project like
mine, that's looking at

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:16.000
the turn of the century,
I'm looking at for instance,

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:18.400
weaving and textile implements.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:21.030
Particularly because female weavers

00:32:21.030 --> 00:32:23.170
as they were mostly
female during this time

00:32:23.170 --> 00:32:27.019
really don't kind of emerge
in a typical study of botany

00:32:27.019 --> 00:32:27.852
in the Philippines

00:32:27.852 --> 00:32:31.230
and yet their practices were
almost entirely plant-based.

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:32.900
I mean these were plant based technologies

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:35.210
where they were relying
on and not just dyes,

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:38.170
but also loon technologies as well.

00:32:38.170 --> 00:32:41.160
And so how do then read
these materials for instance

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:45.760
as vernacular knowledge
plants that are still thriving

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:47.870
if not expanding during this period

00:32:47.870 --> 00:32:50.160
but again are somewhat obscured by only

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:52.640
an Imperial botany narrative at the time.

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:55.840
I think the sampaguita work
also is important in this

00:32:55.840 --> 00:32:58.210
especially if we're thinking
about anti-colonial politics

00:32:58.210 --> 00:33:00.180
that are developing or at least politics

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:04.130
of self-determination that
emerged during this period.

00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:06.790
And I'm also looking in
terms of nomenclature

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:08.770
'cause that's what she also brought up

00:33:08.770 --> 00:33:12.530
at how plant varietals,
varietals of rice in particular.

00:33:12.530 --> 00:33:14.872
And I've mentioned
this, I think when Megan

00:33:14.872 --> 00:33:17.397
was in a different talk
about how rice varietals

00:33:17.397 --> 00:33:19.359
are abandoned in the Philippines

00:33:19.359 --> 00:33:22.050
and how as we currently know
it sort of the Latin name

00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:23.810
Oryza Sativa, right?

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:26.870
For rice existed in the Philippines

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:28.200
botanists were using that

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:30.620
and yet botanists didn't know what to do

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:34.530
with the dozens of rice
varietals that had been present,

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:35.687
that had local names.

00:33:35.687 --> 00:33:38.790
And so for me, I'm really
curious about the epistemologies

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:43.040
of plants ordering that
were very divergent from

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:45.710
Leneyan botany, instead of
focusing on for instance

00:33:45.710 --> 00:33:49.070
morphological characteristics
that define a relationship.

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:52.880
How are people in the colony
at the time using things

00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:57.880
like touch, taste, feel
a rice in the mouth after

00:33:57.970 --> 00:33:59.500
or before cooking, right?

00:33:59.500 --> 00:34:02.200
The stage of a rice plant's growth,

00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:03.640
where it is in it;s cycle,

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:07.790
how do all of those names
actually reveal an incompleteness

00:34:09.105 --> 00:34:11.370
in Leneyan botany, and
it's in that incompleteness

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:14.190
of Leneyan botany that I
think we find a way cooler

00:34:14.190 --> 00:34:17.280
local history to tell
through plant knowledge.

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:18.343
So thanks Cresa.

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:27.490
- Such fascinating discussions,
Denise Margaret Mathias

00:34:27.490 --> 00:34:29.310
I believe you have a question?

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:32.180
- Yes so good afternoon,
everyone or good evening

00:34:32.180 --> 00:34:33.013
from where I am.

00:34:33.886 --> 00:34:36.070
I'm from the Institute for
Social Ecological Research

00:34:36.070 --> 00:34:38.158
in Bonn, Germany.

00:34:38.158 --> 00:34:41.680
So Kat, I was just wondering
about when you were doing

00:34:41.680 --> 00:34:44.350
your archival work whether
there was also a list

00:34:44.350 --> 00:34:46.300
of introduced species.

00:34:46.300 --> 00:34:50.330
So I'm quite interested, like to map out

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:53.403
when did this introduced species such as

00:34:53.403 --> 00:34:57.892
(speaks in foreign language)
come to the Philippines

00:34:57.892 --> 00:35:00.630
and yeah if you've seen that.

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:02.230
Yeah, thank you.

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:04.620
- Yeah you know, I have
to credit some of the work

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:06.690
of historical botanists in the Philippians

00:35:06.690 --> 00:35:09.300
that are doing this right
now that are really tracking

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:11.290
the introduction of particular species.

00:35:11.290 --> 00:35:13.456
Domingo Medulin in particular,

00:35:13.456 --> 00:35:16.100
perhaps the most prolific
historical botanist

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:18.970
has been doing this from
the early Spanish Imperial

00:35:18.970 --> 00:35:22.610
colonial period, by the late
19th century, we do see this

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:26.640
in ledgers of exchange
between the botanical garden

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:29.050
in Manila and other botanical gardens

00:35:29.050 --> 00:35:32.897
that are present for
instance in Hong Kong,

00:35:32.897 --> 00:35:37.120
that are in Singapore and
in the Dutch East Indies.

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:37.990
It's hard to know though

00:35:37.990 --> 00:35:40.740
I mean, I think I haven't
done enough critical work

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:42.530
to look at the introduction
of particular species

00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:45.240
but we do know at the
very least at this time

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:48.440
that there is a much more a
acclimatization work in place

00:35:48.440 --> 00:35:50.450
to try to raise other tropical species

00:35:50.450 --> 00:35:52.520
on the garden grounds.

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:54.320
And so those ledgers there in Madrid,

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:56.801
Denise if you're ever interested in them

00:35:56.801 --> 00:35:58.320
I can even try to dig some
up for you to look through

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:01.670
that might be able to start
placing some of these plants.

00:36:01.670 --> 00:36:04.063
But I would say the much earlier kind of

00:36:04.063 --> 00:36:06.110
introduction of plants

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:08.219
you'd have to look maybe at the work

00:36:08.219 --> 00:36:10.400
of Domingo Medulin really,
really excellent stuff

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:12.150
but we can definitely talk offline.

00:36:15.650 --> 00:36:16.700
- Great, thank you.

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:21.700
Michael Van coming in from the
West coast as well I believe.

00:36:23.060 --> 00:36:24.510
it's good to see you again.

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:27.143
(talking over each other)

00:36:27.143 --> 00:36:29.000
- [Michael] Yes I am (laughs).

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:31.190
Thank you so much for this great

00:36:31.190 --> 00:36:35.724
I'm committed to stand here

00:36:35.724 --> 00:36:38.260
(murmurs) that I have
not read the dissertation

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:40.314
and I'm also gonna commit the sin

00:36:40.314 --> 00:36:42.360
of asking a question
that like is something

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:44.330
that I'm obsessed with in my own research

00:36:44.330 --> 00:36:49.088
but is there a discourse
on invasive species?

00:36:49.088 --> 00:36:51.830
Yeah that resonates with nationalism.

00:36:51.830 --> 00:36:56.230
And is there also as a sub-colonially

00:36:56.230 --> 00:36:59.150
any understanding of Magellan exchange

00:37:00.600 --> 00:37:03.940
colonialism facilitating not plants

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:06.940
that are being acclimatized
but plants that are invading

00:37:06.940 --> 00:37:08.373
and going out of control.

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:10.463
- Oh yeah.

00:37:11.532 --> 00:37:12.930
Great, thank you so much Michael.

00:37:12.930 --> 00:37:16.500
And hi, you know, that doesn't appear

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:19.620
I can't say that in the
texts that I've worked with

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:20.570
from that time period

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:24.080
both that are being produced
by botanists, but then also

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:26.450
among intellectuals who
are writing on plant life

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:29.140
they're not really commenting on invasion.

00:37:29.140 --> 00:37:31.190
But they're either using that language

00:37:31.190 --> 00:37:33.770
of plants that are coming
in and certainly not

00:37:33.770 --> 00:37:35.590
sort of tying back all the way to Magellan

00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:38.140
is not what I necessarily found.

00:37:38.140 --> 00:37:41.090
Although I will say that
that kind of discourse

00:37:41.090 --> 00:37:43.305
has come up in a more contemporary sense.

00:37:43.305 --> 00:37:46.150
I mean, actually tying back
to the sampaguita, right?

00:37:46.150 --> 00:37:49.172
I mean, the sampaguita
itself is not necessarily

00:37:49.172 --> 00:37:51.110
a native of the Philippines.

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:52.810
It's not endemic to the Philippines rather

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:55.804
it's actually endemic to South Asia.

00:37:55.804 --> 00:37:59.960
And so even in the question of nativeness

00:37:59.960 --> 00:38:01.110
of this plant, right?

00:38:01.110 --> 00:38:03.200
That sort of gets thrown into the air

00:38:03.200 --> 00:38:06.220
in particular with debates
about if another plant

00:38:06.220 --> 00:38:07.420
should actually be positioned

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:08.877
as the national plant of the Philippines.

00:38:08.877 --> 00:38:12.072
And this has happened with
an orchid from Mindanal

00:38:12.072 --> 00:38:17.057
but this talk about
invasion versus nativeness,

00:38:17.057 --> 00:38:19.084
as you know Michael can become
really dangerous territory

00:38:19.084 --> 00:38:22.900
especially as how plants can
be positioned in rhetoric

00:38:22.900 --> 00:38:26.190
that has been easily alleged
against people, right?

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:28.030
And so I think even when
I sort of think about

00:38:28.030 --> 00:38:30.470
more contemporary debates
and how I place that

00:38:30.470 --> 00:38:33.470
into turn of the century
