00:50:37.998 --> 00:50:41.240
Had I had more time with the dissertation

00:50:41.240 --> 00:50:44.750
and this is really the
objective for the manuscript

00:50:44.750 --> 00:50:47.570
is to become a little bit
more methodologically creative

00:50:47.570 --> 00:50:48.640
going about these sources.

00:50:48.640 --> 00:50:50.933
And I got to this a little
bit with Cresa's question

00:50:50.933 --> 00:50:54.080
how am I looking at different
source materials then

00:50:54.080 --> 00:50:57.419
for newer archives to
bring out other individuals

00:50:57.419 --> 00:50:59.973
who were carrying plant
knowledge at the time?

00:51:01.160 --> 00:51:03.962
Finding voice can sometimes
be a lofty endeavor.

00:51:03.962 --> 00:51:07.600
I think giving voice to particular actors

00:51:08.691 --> 00:51:12.360
is one that requires a lot
of ethical responsibility

00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:14.290
that I don't take lightly.

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:16.940
But when I think of for
instance, weavers at the time

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:18.890
who were certainly
carrying plant knowledge

00:51:18.890 --> 00:51:21.560
I'm pushing myself to look
at these different sources

00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:24.570
non-textual sources for trying to map out

00:51:24.570 --> 00:51:26.820
what that plant monology may have been.

00:51:26.820 --> 00:51:29.248
Another group of actors would
definitely be collectors

00:51:29.248 --> 00:51:31.380
plant collectors, local guides.

00:51:31.380 --> 00:51:35.050
These were by and large
people that were hired in situ

00:51:35.050 --> 00:51:37.610
to assist colonial botanists and foresters

00:51:37.610 --> 00:51:39.560
with wood collecting or with surveying.

00:51:40.720 --> 00:51:42.510
Local guides and collectors sort of appear

00:51:42.510 --> 00:51:44.490
in the dissertation as
you're probably familiar with

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:47.490
Maximo Ramos, who becomes
the main collector

00:51:47.490 --> 00:51:51.070
for the US colonial
outfit alongside other men

00:51:51.070 --> 00:51:53.470
who were working for
the Bureau of Science.

00:51:53.470 --> 00:51:55.760
Again in their archives
are really disparate

00:51:55.760 --> 00:51:58.780
I mean, they're found sort
of just mentioned in passing

00:51:58.780 --> 00:52:02.210
on places like J store plants
through Clemons archive

00:52:02.210 --> 00:52:04.260
through some of Meryl's archives as well.

00:52:04.260 --> 00:52:06.620
And so even piecing
together their stories,

00:52:06.620 --> 00:52:08.360
what their life may have been

00:52:08.360 --> 00:52:10.390
the different kinds of
knowledge they brought

00:52:10.390 --> 00:52:14.930
into the institutions as people
from a particular location

00:52:14.930 --> 00:52:17.180
in the colony of people who had decades

00:52:17.180 --> 00:52:18.660
of collecting experience.

00:52:18.660 --> 00:52:20.440
Those are the kinds of actors
I really wanna bring out

00:52:20.440 --> 00:52:22.160
in the manuscript.

00:52:22.160 --> 00:52:23.670
I don't wanna lose sight of Garcia

00:52:23.670 --> 00:52:25.630
I still think that he's
a really important figure

00:52:25.630 --> 00:52:26.463
to bring out

00:52:26.463 --> 00:52:28.500
and I think you also bring
up an important point.

00:52:28.500 --> 00:52:30.790
He's also still kind of
more of an illustrator

00:52:30.790 --> 00:52:34.720
into larger figure that
becomes important even in

00:52:34.720 --> 00:52:36.820
sort of the story of
botany and the Philippines.

00:52:36.820 --> 00:52:38.560
And so I definitely don't wanna lose him

00:52:38.560 --> 00:52:40.550
but I hope that sort of
answered some of your questions

00:52:40.550 --> 00:52:42.922
for how I might start to
think a little bit more

00:52:42.922 --> 00:52:46.900
about mining, different
archives for botanical knowledge

00:52:46.900 --> 00:52:49.543
where the colonial archive
is very insufficient.

00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:53.571
Post 1945 super interesting time,

00:52:53.571 --> 00:52:55.480
really cool stuff going on.

00:52:55.480 --> 00:52:58.250
I've started collecting
some oral histories

00:52:58.250 --> 00:53:01.260
with botanists who trained
or conducted research

00:53:01.260 --> 00:53:03.280
during the cold war and what I love

00:53:03.280 --> 00:53:05.420
and I'll just kind of
give you a snippet here.

00:53:05.420 --> 00:53:07.410
When I'm asked, how did the cold war

00:53:07.410 --> 00:53:09.840
impact your research by and large?

00:53:09.840 --> 00:53:11.983
They say it didn't.

00:53:14.272 --> 00:53:16.070
And so I thought to myself
like, okay I'm gonna begin

00:53:16.070 --> 00:53:18.420
the next book by saying
the cold war didn't happen

00:53:18.420 --> 00:53:22.671
for Philippine botanists
and really dive into

00:53:22.671 --> 00:53:25.620
where these comments are
coming from because certainly

00:53:25.620 --> 00:53:29.200
botany was exploding
in the post-war period,

00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:32.820
it was really ramping
up even under Marcos.

00:53:32.820 --> 00:53:36.570
And so the way that I want to
attend to this next project

00:53:36.570 --> 00:53:40.931
is looking at how plants and
the scientists who studied them

00:53:40.931 --> 00:53:44.040
are kind of also partaking in
sort of this political project

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:46.740
of new Philippine
independence in some way.

00:53:46.740 --> 00:53:49.270
And what I also think
is really exciting is

00:53:49.270 --> 00:53:51.860
kind of taking on the
notion of agential realism

00:53:51.860 --> 00:53:54.710
or plant agency and all of us too.

00:53:54.710 --> 00:53:57.725
So how do we start to think
about plants as sort of being

00:53:57.725 --> 00:54:02.170
historical actors alongside
these plant scientists?

00:54:02.170 --> 00:54:05.210
And I think that's a
kind of a fun exercise

00:54:05.210 --> 00:54:06.730
to start to imagine.

00:54:06.730 --> 00:54:08.680
And I don't mean to say that I'm arguing

00:54:08.680 --> 00:54:12.166
about plants necessarily
have sentient decision-making

00:54:12.166 --> 00:54:15.150
powers over the actors, you know

00:54:15.150 --> 00:54:17.080
that I'm looking at in this time period.

00:54:17.080 --> 00:54:19.530
But I'm wanting to really
push us to think much more

00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:23.770
about plant growing
patterns, plants movement

00:54:23.770 --> 00:54:25.280
across the archipelago.

00:54:25.280 --> 00:54:28.270
As you know, people at UC Santa
Cruz are really fond to do

00:54:28.270 --> 00:54:30.240
is just start thinking with plants

00:54:30.240 --> 00:54:33.270
start writing with plants,
to allow for a very different

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:34.990
kind of history to come out,

00:54:34.990 --> 00:54:37.140
especially in the cold war
period in the Philippines

00:54:37.140 --> 00:54:39.270
as you know that's really been dominated

00:54:39.270 --> 00:54:40.670
by a particular set of literature

00:54:40.670 --> 00:54:42.230
that's focused on Marcos.

00:54:42.230 --> 00:54:43.540
I think that's focused really well

00:54:43.540 --> 00:54:45.539
on domestic and transnational protest

00:54:45.539 --> 00:54:48.476
but it hasn't maybe done enough
yet to look at the science

00:54:48.476 --> 00:54:49.700
and what science is doing.

00:54:49.700 --> 00:54:52.460
And I know Kirby on the
call is doing that too

00:54:52.460 --> 00:54:55.093
so I'm really excited
about the post 1945 work.

00:54:58.560 --> 00:55:00.280
- Thanks for all these
stimulating questions

