I tell everyone at this
time that I'm gonna keep

00:55:07.570 --> 00:55:10.770
the session open for
another 15 minutes or so

00:55:10.770 --> 00:55:14.849
but I also give permission
to those who need to leave

00:55:14.849 --> 00:55:16.564
at one o'clock to do so gracefully.

00:55:16.564 --> 00:55:18.590
And I just wanna thank everyone

00:55:18.590 --> 00:55:21.400
for being with us until
now, but we will continue

00:55:21.400 --> 00:55:24.680
we have a few more questions
after about 1:15 or so

00:55:24.680 --> 00:55:26.060
I'll turn off the recording

00:55:26.060 --> 00:55:29.333
and then we can have an open
chit chat session as well.

00:55:30.436 --> 00:55:31.914
So stick around if you have the time

00:55:31.914 --> 00:55:34.020
but you're also free to go if you need to.

00:55:34.020 --> 00:55:36.643
So we have a question
from Tri Phuong, Tri.

00:55:38.160 --> 00:55:41.031
- Hello, doctor professor Gutierrez.

00:55:41.031 --> 00:55:44.660
Thank you for a wonderfully stimulating

00:55:44.660 --> 00:55:46.743
conversation that we've been having.

00:55:46.743 --> 00:55:49.573
My question is very brief.

00:55:51.480 --> 00:55:55.056
I wonder if you could speak to,
or speak on or share with us

00:55:55.056 --> 00:56:00.056
how you happened upon or
discovered intimately botany

00:56:01.482 --> 00:56:05.690
as a heuristic and also
a modality for unpacking

00:56:05.690 --> 00:56:10.143
and studying Filipino
history, culture and society.

00:56:10.143 --> 00:56:11.240
That's all.

00:56:11.240 --> 00:56:13.607
Yeah, thanks Tri good to see you (laughs).

00:56:17.070 --> 00:56:20.400
You know, yeah, I can answer
this a few different ways

00:56:20.400 --> 00:56:22.080
I mean, I think in terms
of my own professional

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:25.060
intellectual trajectory
before I entered into academia

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:27.610
I was in public health for many years.

00:56:27.610 --> 00:56:30.300
And I thought that when I
entered into my doctoral program

00:56:30.300 --> 00:56:32.730
for those of you who are maybe
still in the starting stages

00:56:32.730 --> 00:56:34.760
of figuring out the PhD project

00:56:34.760 --> 00:56:37.560
I thought I was gonna work
on colonial public health.

00:56:37.560 --> 00:56:40.630
A good way to begin in that
is through medical botany

00:56:40.630 --> 00:56:43.330
which becomes a really important facet

00:56:43.330 --> 00:56:45.450
of Philippine public health.

00:56:45.450 --> 00:56:48.150
I worked with Laura Nader,
who is also an anthropologist

00:56:48.150 --> 00:56:50.020
who's worked with Erik a little bit

00:56:50.020 --> 00:56:51.520
and he really encouraged me to look at

00:56:51.520 --> 00:56:53.712
Philippine medicinal plants.

00:56:53.712 --> 00:56:57.390
And that really took me
back to my dad actually

00:56:57.390 --> 00:56:59.440
because I had known
somewhere down the line

00:56:59.440 --> 00:57:01.690
he had written on medicinal
plants and Philippians.

00:57:01.690 --> 00:57:03.330
And so I thought, you know what,

00:57:03.330 --> 00:57:04.420
let me just ask my dad

00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:07.303
about the avocado you know (laughs)

00:57:07.303 --> 00:57:09.406
and like it's material medical quality.

00:57:09.406 --> 00:57:13.620
And that just started some
pretty amazing conversations

00:57:13.620 --> 00:57:16.210
and discoveries for me and
sort of the gaps in literature

00:57:16.210 --> 00:57:18.570
about the history of
botany and the Philippians.

00:57:18.570 --> 00:57:21.070
The lineage of botanists
is very very narrow

00:57:21.070 --> 00:57:23.540
you can really track who learned from whom

00:57:23.540 --> 00:57:26.020
especially over the course
of the 20th century.

00:57:26.020 --> 00:57:29.190
And as I started to sort of
envision my dad in that lineage

00:57:29.190 --> 00:57:31.580
and he certainly is in that lineage

00:57:31.580 --> 00:57:34.320
I started to become interested
in sort of tracking back,

00:57:34.320 --> 00:57:38.390
further back to find kind of
the debates within botany,

00:57:38.390 --> 00:57:40.717
the gossip, you know in botany.

00:57:40.717 --> 00:57:42.980
But the really amazing stories that reveal

00:57:42.980 --> 00:57:47.330
parts of Philippine history,
otherwise maybe obscured

00:57:47.330 --> 00:57:50.150
or not as clearly seen
through something like botany

00:57:50.150 --> 00:57:51.540
in the past.

00:57:51.540 --> 00:57:54.300
And so it's a very personal, I think

00:57:54.300 --> 00:57:56.840
and also intellectual trajectory of mine.

00:57:56.840 --> 00:58:00.780
And I'm really always, proud
of saying that that much

00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:04.400
of my work or like love letters
to my dad for a scientist

00:58:04.400 --> 00:58:07.013
who I think has been pretty
peripheral in many ways

00:58:07.013 --> 00:58:09.540
even in his sort of experience

00:58:09.540 --> 00:58:11.000
with the aspirin in the United States.

00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:14.476
And so I think that's kind
of my short long answer

00:58:14.476 --> 00:58:18.010
to why botany now, but I
think it's still opening up

00:58:18.010 --> 00:58:20.867
greater investigations again,
of other plant sciences

00:58:20.867 --> 00:58:23.168
and other ways of thinking
about the environment

00:58:23.168 --> 00:58:25.340
that are feeling very natural

00:58:25.340 --> 00:58:27.240
to the progression of this trajectory.

00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:32.750
- Thanks for that.

00:58:32.750 --> 00:58:35.133
Harry, go ahead and ask your question.

00:58:38.140 --> 00:58:40.120
- Thank you professor Gutierrez

00:58:40.120 --> 00:58:41.920
for being so generous and thoughtful

00:58:43.110 --> 00:58:44.300
in your exchange with us today.

00:58:44.300 --> 00:58:47.340
Actually my question, you
touched on it a little bit

00:58:47.340 --> 00:58:50.250
in your thinking of over and around

00:58:50.250 --> 00:58:51.720
lots of historical actors.

00:58:51.720 --> 00:58:56.310
But I was wanting to ask a
little bit about perspective

00:58:56.310 --> 00:59:00.873
'cause it strikes me that
when we look at plots,

00:59:04.592 --> 00:59:08.924
we kind of glean alternative a
new or different perspectives

00:59:08.924 --> 00:59:11.510
on cartography, on
questions of nationhood,

00:59:11.510 --> 00:59:14.423
on regionality and on history.

00:59:15.660 --> 00:59:20.656
Looking at plants teaches us
to look at Malaysia and see

00:59:20.656 --> 00:59:25.540
the kind of relationships
that create different grounds

00:59:25.540 --> 00:59:27.040
to like think of your history.

00:59:28.060 --> 00:59:31.341
But also kind of, I've been
thinking about how plants

00:59:31.341 --> 00:59:33.860
looking at plants, teaches us to look

00:59:33.860 --> 00:59:37.150
at history from the ground up
and this kind of like working

00:59:37.150 --> 00:59:40.510
like a methodological kind of
insistence on like a visuality

00:59:40.510 --> 00:59:42.803
that's like from below.

00:59:44.800 --> 00:59:48.970
But then that led me to think
a little bit about perspective

00:59:48.970 --> 00:59:51.380
and thinking about plants,
not just as objects of study

00:59:51.380 --> 00:59:53.133
but as subjects of studying with.

00:59:57.040 --> 00:59:59.060
You know, plant knowledge
being not something

00:59:59.060 --> 01:00:02.123
that botanists bear but
if they need the knowledge

01:00:02.123 --> 01:00:04.330
that plants bear like
plant knowledge being like

01:00:04.330 --> 01:00:06.270
plant epistemology.

01:00:06.270 --> 01:00:07.850
So I guess my question

01:00:07.850 --> 01:00:09.970
I'm struggling to know
exactly where to go with this

01:00:09.970 --> 01:00:12.525
and maybe it's just a prompt
to you to speak a bit more

01:00:12.525 --> 01:00:13.670
about plants and historical activists.

01:00:13.670 --> 01:00:15.510
But I'm also wondering
about why they're thinking

01:00:15.510 --> 01:00:18.030
relationing with plants
or thinking through plants

01:00:18.030 --> 01:00:22.783
as subjects of (murmurs) can
lead to maybe kind of like

01:00:24.390 --> 01:00:28.882
unsettling of like the visual
as this kind of like motive

01:00:28.882 --> 01:00:33.882
of kind of like rational
domination and like transparent

01:00:33.910 --> 01:00:35.540
understanding of history.

01:00:35.540 --> 01:00:39.770
And what is your perspective
on the idea of the perspective

01:00:39.770 --> 01:00:44.770
of the kind of ways that
plants can intervene

01:00:47.590 --> 01:00:49.823
and interact and produce
history themselves.

01:00:51.430 --> 01:00:53.060
- Yes oh, it's so cool.

01:00:53.060 --> 01:00:54.030
Yeah no thank you, Harry.

01:00:54.030 --> 01:00:55.730
I think that that's yeah,

01:00:55.730 --> 01:00:57.920
I can share a little bit of some ideas

01:00:57.920 --> 01:00:59.400
that I've been sort of teasing through

01:00:59.400 --> 01:01:01.670
to answer this question for myself.

01:01:01.670 --> 01:01:05.140
Think it's a very fun
one and I can just share

01:01:05.140 --> 01:01:06.820
and just say to you that kind of idea

01:01:06.820 --> 01:01:09.480
of ground up visual
reality and perspective

01:01:09.480 --> 01:01:11.530
and methodologies it's so great.

01:01:11.530 --> 01:01:13.300
I never thought of it like that,

01:01:13.300 --> 01:01:14.943
I never thought of it like that

01:01:14.943 --> 01:01:17.690
and I think that's important for work

01:01:17.690 --> 01:01:20.220
for how we might start
to just, again undo maybe

01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:22.910
a little bit more of a
human centered approach

01:01:22.910 --> 01:01:24.770
even in a field like history, right?

01:01:24.770 --> 01:01:26.650
Which I think some fields of history

01:01:26.650 --> 01:01:29.651
have done a marvelous job
at sort of undoing, right?

01:01:29.651 --> 01:01:33.040
When we're thinking about plant agency

01:01:33.040 --> 01:01:35.320
I go about this in one particular way

01:01:35.320 --> 01:01:38.010
would be a Imelda Marcos toad lily.

01:01:38.010 --> 01:01:41.180
And it's again, an exercise in
just suspending for a moment

01:01:41.180 --> 01:01:43.350
the idea that humans had control

01:01:43.350 --> 01:01:46.670
of the entire situation
in the discovery naming

01:01:46.670 --> 01:01:49.900
and promotion of this
Imelda Marcos toad lily

01:01:49.900 --> 01:01:53.890
which had been discovered in
the 1970s and named after her.

01:01:53.890 --> 01:01:57.480
In particular, I'm curious
about how this one Lily

01:01:57.480 --> 01:02:01.200
which is actually, known
to grow around Taiwan

01:02:01.200 --> 01:02:04.550
and Southern China
somehow manages to end up

01:02:04.550 --> 01:02:06.970
at the Southern snip of
the Philippine archipelago.

01:02:06.970 --> 01:02:08.950
Nowhere seen anywhere
else in the archipelago

01:02:08.950 --> 01:02:12.381
but is somehow in this
location found among

01:02:12.381 --> 01:02:15.540
the purported cave dwellers
that thacadye people

01:02:15.540 --> 01:02:17.590
if you're familiar with Philippian

01:02:17.590 --> 01:02:18.423
at the biological industry

01:02:18.423 --> 01:02:21.030
the thasadye were purported
to be cave dwelling people

01:02:21.030 --> 01:02:23.740
that were discovered
suddenly by the Marcos regime

01:02:25.290 --> 01:02:27.700
this lily was discovered among them.

01:02:27.700 --> 01:02:30.460
And it was named after Imelda
Marcos to kind of uphold

01:02:30.460 --> 01:02:33.793
the Marcos's legacy in
supporting indigenous peoples.

01:02:35.110 --> 01:02:36.830
We find out later in history, of course

01:02:36.830 --> 01:02:39.090
that the thasadye were a huge hoax.

01:02:39.090 --> 01:02:40.890
We had discovered of
course, that the Marcos's

01:02:40.890 --> 01:02:43.500
were plundering hoaxes themselves.

01:02:43.500 --> 01:02:46.260
But I questioned for a moment and I wonder

01:02:46.260 --> 01:02:49.810
that this one plant that's
able to jump somehow

01:02:49.810 --> 01:02:53.440
from Taiwan all the way to
the edge of the Philippines

01:02:53.440 --> 01:02:55.590
in a very, very remote location,

01:02:55.590 --> 01:02:58.073
somehow participated in the hoax as well.

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:02.610
I mean, it becomes subsumed
in this kind of laughing stock

01:03:02.610 --> 01:03:05.010
that the Marcos's become after
they're pushed out of office

01:03:05.010 --> 01:03:07.400
in 1985.

01:03:07.400 --> 01:03:08.880
And I like to think about that.

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:12.110
I mean, how can we think of
a plant who is simply working

01:03:12.110 --> 01:03:15.800
outside of its growing range
is also contributing to this?

01:03:15.800 --> 01:03:18.760
And that's where I think plant
agency sort of intervenes

01:03:18.760 --> 01:03:22.010
a little bit more when we
think a little bit about

01:03:22.010 --> 01:03:26.760
how plants are sort of
actively creating communities

01:03:26.760 --> 01:03:28.250
of people themselves, right?

01:03:28.250 --> 01:03:31.030
Ontologically speaking,
communities of botanists

01:03:31.030 --> 01:03:33.973
can't exist without plants, right?

01:03:35.376 --> 01:03:37.040
How do those constitutions
then change over time?

01:03:37.040 --> 01:03:40.020
How was a particular moment
like the cold war Philippians

01:03:40.020 --> 01:03:41.750
being constituted in science

01:03:41.750 --> 01:03:45.050
not only because of political
social, intellectual forces

01:03:45.050 --> 01:03:47.540
but because of the
plants that are presented

01:03:47.540 --> 01:03:50.236
or presenting themselves to botanists too.

01:03:50.236 --> 01:03:53.220
And I think that's a shift
in perspective that allows

01:03:53.220 --> 01:03:56.980
for a very different kind of
storytelling that allows again

01:03:56.980 --> 01:03:59.030
for a little bit of a de-centered approach

01:03:59.030 --> 01:04:00.821
to the human actor.

01:04:00.821 --> 01:04:02.733
I hope that answers your question Harry.

01:04:05.550 --> 01:04:07.504
- Such a fascinating conversation.

01:04:07.504 --> 01:04:10.960
I'll turn it to Thuy
for the last question.

01:04:10.960 --> 01:04:15.887
And then after that, we'll turn
to our informal conversation

01:04:15.887 --> 01:04:17.010
and I'll stop the recording

01:04:17.010 --> 01:04:18.860
after the reply to the go ahead Thuy.

01:04:20.128 --> 01:04:21.690
- Okay thank you, professor Harms

01:04:21.690 --> 01:04:24.880
and thank you professor I'm not sure

01:04:24.880 --> 01:04:27.062
I can pronounce your name correctly.

01:04:27.062 --> 01:04:28.580
Gutei?

01:04:28.580 --> 01:04:32.239
Okay so I'm Vietnamese,
I'm not Filipinos (laughs)

01:04:32.239 --> 01:04:37.239
and I'm not in life science
environmentalist or something

01:04:37.980 --> 01:04:41.450
but I have long time
working in the family union.

01:04:41.450 --> 01:04:46.450
So I work very closely with
farmers and I see your research

01:04:46.450 --> 01:04:51.450
I guess the reason (murmurs)
I'm waiting for the book.

01:04:55.476 --> 01:04:59.690
I have a question about like,
when I work with farmers

01:04:59.690 --> 01:05:04.180
and interesting in development,
I care about development.

01:05:04.180 --> 01:05:06.150
So when I work with the farmers

01:05:07.500 --> 01:05:10.330
I talk a lot with the
farmers in the Mekong Delta.

01:05:10.330 --> 01:05:14.320
A lot of them complain
about like the botany

01:05:14.320 --> 01:05:16.423
like that's very interesting reason.

01:05:17.880 --> 01:05:22.880
There is about like 19th
history, early 20th century

01:05:23.920 --> 01:05:26.400
not at the present, not about development

01:05:27.830 --> 01:05:31.390
but when I talk with the
farmers, they come in about

01:05:31.390 --> 01:05:36.390
a lot of firsts kind of
disappear and a lot of grass

01:05:37.270 --> 01:05:42.270
even grass and plants, we don't
see anymore in the fields.

01:05:43.412 --> 01:05:48.157
And so growing up in the
village so my family,

01:05:50.709 --> 01:05:51.913
my parents are farmers.

01:05:53.181 --> 01:05:55.081
So my entire childhood in the gardens.

01:05:57.190 --> 01:06:00.790
So what about like 20 years now?

01:06:00.790 --> 01:06:04.450
I don't see a lot of plants because of

01:06:05.656 --> 01:06:06.970
like the developments

01:06:06.970 --> 01:06:11.500
people don't work in the
farms in agriculture.

01:06:11.500 --> 01:06:16.087
So they don't have farmers to work

01:06:16.087 --> 01:06:18.850
and to pick the grass like I did before.

01:06:18.850 --> 01:06:22.420
So they use a lot of
chemicals in the Mekong Delta,

01:06:22.420 --> 01:06:25.473
in my village in the South of Vietnam.

01:06:28.399 --> 01:06:31.960
So to me development affect the botany

01:06:33.150 --> 01:06:37.260
like a lot and even in my garden now

01:06:37.260 --> 01:06:39.540
my mom had to grow grass.

01:06:41.918 --> 01:06:45.916
(mumbles) We have a lot of grass before

01:06:45.916 --> 01:06:50.916
and when I came here I
went through the path

01:06:51.000 --> 01:06:53.915
I see a lot of grass that I saw before

01:06:53.915 --> 01:06:57.000
and I don't see it now in Vietnam.

01:06:57.000 --> 01:07:00.630
I only see it in the botanical garden

01:07:00.630 --> 01:07:02.963
when I went to the botanicals

01:07:04.061 --> 01:07:07.160
I saw a lot of them in
the botanical gardens

01:07:07.160 --> 01:07:08.900
that the farmers complain to me

01:07:08.900 --> 01:07:10.770
that they don't see it anymore.

01:07:10.770 --> 01:07:14.750
So do you have any plan
that you do research

01:07:14.750 --> 01:07:19.750
like the effect of development
botany in the Philippines

01:07:20.800 --> 01:07:22.670
or in Southeast Asia?

01:07:22.670 --> 01:07:25.440
I think Vietnam have that too.

01:07:25.440 --> 01:07:29.030
So that's just my question
about your future plans

01:07:29.030 --> 01:07:33.343
because I don't have knowledge
about your best (mumbles)

01:07:33.343 --> 01:07:36.163
and I haven't read your work.

01:07:37.120 --> 01:07:38.867
Okay, that's my question.

01:07:38.867 --> 01:07:42.560
- Yeah thank you so much Thuy
I appreciate the question.

01:07:42.560 --> 01:07:44.870
I'm not sure if Megan
is still on the call too

01:07:44.870 --> 01:07:49.870
but I actually think that,
even if I were work in history

01:07:50.676 --> 01:07:54.610
I absolutely find myself
responsible to being responsive

01:07:54.610 --> 01:07:58.102
to contemporary conditions
that might very well impact

01:07:58.102 --> 01:08:00.510
the environments of Southeast Asia.

01:08:00.510 --> 01:08:04.040
I only ask if Megan's on the
call because I'm making a plan

01:08:04.040 --> 01:08:07.300
for the center for Southeast
Asian coastal interactions

01:08:07.300 --> 01:08:10.060
which brings together
different disciplines

01:08:10.060 --> 01:08:13.180
including history, anthropology
and the life sciences

01:08:13.180 --> 01:08:15.990
to attend to these bigger
questions that really confronting

01:08:15.990 --> 01:08:18.150
Southeast Asia today.

01:08:18.150 --> 01:08:21.180
Much of the focus right now
at the center has been on

01:08:21.180 --> 01:08:24.010
coastal life, for instance mangroves

01:08:24.010 --> 01:08:26.200
and mangroves degradation

01:08:26.200 --> 01:08:30.380
but then also mangrove rejuvenation.

01:08:30.380 --> 01:08:33.660
And ability to regenerate in spite of

01:08:33.660 --> 01:08:35.910
the climatological
changes that are impacting

01:08:36.926 --> 01:08:38.470
Southeast Asia in ways that
I don't even have to explain

01:08:38.470 --> 01:08:40.630
because I think we're pretty
well aware even on this call

01:08:40.630 --> 01:08:43.810
about what we're confronting
right now in the region.

01:08:43.810 --> 01:08:45.930
I think in terms of the
historical lands that I know

01:08:45.930 --> 01:08:49.650
I'm really excited to try to
offer and to continue to build

01:08:49.650 --> 01:08:51.560
is the question of disappearance

01:08:51.560 --> 01:08:54.023
and for whom disappearance
becomes important?

01:08:55.540 --> 01:08:58.670
And so even when we think of
deforestation, that's happening

01:08:58.670 --> 01:09:01.390
at the turn of the century,
the actors that are

01:09:01.390 --> 01:09:03.567
writing perhaps the most on deforestation

01:09:03.567 --> 01:09:07.670
are colonizing foresters
who become pretty involved

01:09:07.670 --> 01:09:10.100
in why timber trees for
instance are disappearing

01:09:10.100 --> 01:09:11.380
at such a fast rate?

01:09:11.380 --> 01:09:14.180
And become much more
invested in policing forests

01:09:14.180 --> 01:09:16.180
in order to prevent for instance,

01:09:16.180 --> 01:09:19.783
locals from taking down trees
that aren't ready to be feld.

01:09:20.790 --> 01:09:23.810
And so these questions of
development really tie into

01:09:23.810 --> 01:09:27.460
well whose one disappearance
is being valued

01:09:27.460 --> 01:09:29.630
at the expense of what kind of development

01:09:29.630 --> 01:09:31.670
that's happening at the time.

01:09:31.670 --> 01:09:34.250
And then of course there's
excavation, I think as Orven

01:09:34.250 --> 01:09:37.020
and others are really
trying to really insist

01:09:37.020 --> 01:09:40.170
on the different kinds of
perspectives in relationship

01:09:40.170 --> 01:09:41.780
to that disappearance.

01:09:41.780 --> 01:09:43.640
Sometimes it's harder to find an archive

01:09:43.640 --> 01:09:45.780
but I think it's still
all the more relevant.

01:09:45.780 --> 01:09:47.500
And you must maybe look for instance

01:09:47.500 --> 01:09:48.760
at different kinds of sources.

01:09:48.760 --> 01:09:51.440
I think this is where songs,
poetry, particular kinds

01:09:51.440 --> 01:09:54.594
of folklore that we might
be able to track back

01:09:54.594 --> 01:09:56.452
to this time period become important.

01:09:56.452 --> 01:09:58.120
Because then we're able to see
what that discourse might be

01:09:58.120 --> 01:10:00.820
on the fast vanishing
forests that become really

01:10:00.820 --> 01:10:03.090
the preoccupation of colonial botany

01:10:03.090 --> 01:10:04.533
at the turn of the century.

01:10:05.840 --> 01:10:07.570
So I hope that answers your questions

01:10:07.570 --> 01:10:08.960
we should definitely try to see you

01:10:08.960 --> 01:10:11.860
at some Seacoast meetings
that are still online

01:10:11.860 --> 01:10:14.473
and hopefully you can bring
your Vietnam perspective too.

01:10:16.900 --> 01:10:19.510
- Well, thank you professor Gutierrez

01:10:19.510 --> 01:10:21.570
for such a fascinating and generous

01:10:21.570 --> 01:10:24.670
and wide ranging conversation
and for sticking with us

01:10:24.670 --> 01:10:29.030
for so long and entertaining
all of our expansive questions

01:10:29.030 --> 01:10:30.890
it's really been quite fascinating.

01:10:30.890 --> 01:10:32.880
So first I wanna just
have everyone join me

01:10:32.880 --> 01:10:35.550
in thanking you for such a fantastic talk

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:37.600
and give you a round of applause (claps).

